1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,324
Some freight trains in North America are

2
00:00:03,324 --> 00:00:05,734
longer than three kilometers,

3
00:00:05,734 --> 00:00:08,394
almost two miles of steel boxes.

4
00:00:08,574 --> 00:00:10,576
And if you watch one pass,

5
00:00:10,576 --> 00:00:12,579
something strange happens.

6
00:00:12,759 --> 00:00:16,242
Halfway through, there is another locomotive.

7
00:00:16,422 --> 00:00:18,976
Sometimes there is one at the very

8
00:00:18,976 --> 00:00:20,253
end too, pushing.

9
00:00:20,433 --> 00:00:22,256
No crew inside.

10
00:00:22,436 --> 00:00:24,541
It is being driven by radio from

11
00:00:24,541 --> 00:00:25,199
the front.

12
00:00:25,379 --> 00:00:28,630
Today, ten parts of a giant freight

13
00:00:28,630 --> 00:00:31,509
train, and why each one exists.

14
00:00:31,859 --> 00:00:34,801
Freight trains are the quiet giants of

15
00:00:34,801 --> 00:00:35,575
transport.

16
00:00:35,755 --> 00:00:38,253
One train can carry the load of

17
00:00:38,253 --> 00:00:39,784
hundreds of trucks,

18
00:00:39,784 --> 00:00:42,363
using a fraction of the fuel per

19
00:00:42,363 --> 00:00:42,686
ton.

20
00:00:42,866 --> 00:00:45,246
But the longer and heavier a train

21
00:00:45,246 --> 00:00:47,487
gets, the more it fights itself.

22
00:00:47,667 --> 00:00:48,898
The front pulls,

23
00:00:48,898 --> 00:00:50,130
the back pushes,

24
00:00:50,130 --> 00:00:53,055
every coupling stretches and squeezes,

25
00:00:53,055 --> 00:00:55,518
and a mistake can tear the train

26
00:00:55,518 --> 00:00:55,979
apart.

27
00:00:56,159 --> 00:00:59,136
Almost every strange part of a freight

28
00:00:59,136 --> 00:01:02,034
train exists to control those forces.

29
00:01:02,383 --> 00:01:05,025
As always, every fact comes from a

30
00:01:05,025 --> 00:01:08,911
named source, the Federal Railroad Administration,

31
00:01:08,911 --> 00:01:11,941
railway companies, and railway history,

32
00:01:11,941 --> 00:01:14,272
all listed in the description.

33
00:01:14,452 --> 00:01:16,455
Let's start with that engine in the

34
00:01:16,455 --> 00:01:16,855
middle.

35
00:01:17,205 --> 00:01:20,008
Why do long freight trains have locomotives

36
00:01:20,008 --> 00:01:20,921
in the middle?

37
00:01:21,101 --> 00:01:23,632
It is called distributed power.

38
00:01:23,812 --> 00:01:26,015
If all the locomotives are at the

39
00:01:26,015 --> 00:01:28,485
front, the couplers at the front have

40
00:01:28,485 --> 00:01:30,621
to pull the weight of the entire

41
00:01:30,621 --> 00:01:31,021
train.

42
00:01:31,201 --> 00:01:32,273
On a very long,

43
00:01:32,273 --> 00:01:34,775
heavy train, that force can break a

44
00:01:34,775 --> 00:01:35,346
coupler.

45
00:01:35,526 --> 00:01:38,685
Putting some locomotives in the middle or

46
00:01:38,685 --> 00:01:41,151
at the end splits the train into

47
00:01:41,151 --> 00:01:44,311
sections, and each group of engines pulls

48
00:01:44,311 --> 00:01:46,776
or pushes only part of the load.

49
00:01:47,126 --> 00:01:49,530
The remote locomotives have no crew.

50
00:01:49,710 --> 00:01:52,928
The engineer at the front controls them

51
00:01:52,928 --> 00:01:55,981
by radio, and they copy the commands,

52
00:01:55,981 --> 00:01:58,951
or can be set to work independently.

53
00:01:59,131 --> 00:02:02,053
Systems like this were developed in the

54
00:02:02,053 --> 00:02:05,275
nineteen sixties, and today they are common

55
00:02:05,275 --> 00:02:06,399
on long trains.

56
00:02:06,579 --> 00:02:08,634
In curves, they help too.

57
00:02:08,814 --> 00:02:11,104
A long train pulled only from the

58
00:02:11,104 --> 00:02:14,088
front tends to straighten itself across the

59
00:02:14,088 --> 00:02:15,476
inside of the curve,

60
00:02:15,476 --> 00:02:17,835
which can pull cars off the track.

61
00:02:18,184 --> 00:02:20,170
Braking is also better.

62
00:02:20,350 --> 00:02:21,520
On a long train,

63
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:24,300
the air brake signal travels along the

64
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:25,763
pipe from the front,

65
00:02:25,763 --> 00:02:27,958
and it takes time to reach the

66
00:02:27,958 --> 00:02:28,616
last car.

67
00:02:28,796 --> 00:02:31,702
With locomotives in the middle and at

68
00:02:31,702 --> 00:02:34,372
the end, the brakes can be applied

69
00:02:34,372 --> 00:02:36,571
from several points at once,

70
00:02:36,571 --> 00:02:39,241
so the whole train slows down more

71
00:02:39,241 --> 00:02:39,791
evenly.

72
00:02:39,971 --> 00:02:42,951
More engines in more places means a

73
00:02:42,951 --> 00:02:44,057
calmer train.

74
00:02:44,407 --> 00:02:46,032
Why do you hear a chain of

75
00:02:46,032 --> 00:02:48,158
bangs when a freight train starts?

76
00:02:48,338 --> 00:02:51,087
Every coupling between two cars has a

77
00:02:51,087 --> 00:02:53,985
little slack, a few centimeters of free

78
00:02:53,985 --> 00:02:54,653
movement.

79
00:02:54,833 --> 00:02:56,994
When the locomotive starts,

80
00:02:56,994 --> 00:02:59,315
it first pulls the first car,

81
00:02:59,315 --> 00:03:01,236
then the slack runs out,

82
00:03:01,236 --> 00:03:03,397
then the second car starts,

83
00:03:03,397 --> 00:03:04,597
then the third.

84
00:03:04,777 --> 00:03:07,246
With more than one hundred cars,

85
00:03:07,246 --> 00:03:10,256
those few centimeters add up to several

86
00:03:10,256 --> 00:03:12,571
meters, and you hear a wave of

87
00:03:12,571 --> 00:03:14,808
bangs running down the train.

88
00:03:15,159 --> 00:03:17,179
The slack is not a mistake.

89
00:03:17,359 --> 00:03:20,190
It actually helps a locomotive start a

90
00:03:20,190 --> 00:03:22,947
heavy train, because it does not have

91
00:03:22,947 --> 00:03:24,958
to move all the cars at the

92
00:03:24,958 --> 00:03:25,927
same instant.

93
00:03:26,107 --> 00:03:28,754
Behind each coupler there is a spring

94
00:03:28,754 --> 00:03:31,474
and friction system called draft gear,

95
00:03:31,474 --> 00:03:33,549
which cushions those impacts.

96
00:03:33,729 --> 00:03:36,564
Without it, every start and stop would

97
00:03:36,564 --> 00:03:38,802
hammer the cars and the cargo.

98
00:03:39,153 --> 00:03:41,730
But slack can also be dangerous.

99
00:03:41,910 --> 00:03:44,167
If the front of a train slows

100
00:03:44,167 --> 00:03:47,046
down while the back is still pushing,

101
00:03:47,046 --> 00:03:48,447
the slack runs in,

102
00:03:48,447 --> 00:03:50,548
and the cars slam together.

103
00:03:50,728 --> 00:03:52,946
Then if the front speeds up,

104
00:03:52,946 --> 00:03:55,719
the slack runs out and the couplers

105
00:03:55,719 --> 00:03:56,591
snap tight.

106
00:03:56,771 --> 00:04:00,284
Engineers on long freight trains are trained

107
00:04:00,284 --> 00:04:02,360
to manage slack carefully,

108
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:03,957
especially on hills.

109
00:04:04,137 --> 00:04:06,601
Driving a two mile train is a

110
00:04:06,601 --> 00:04:09,234
lot like managing an accordion.

111
00:04:09,585 --> 00:04:11,512
Why is one part of a freight

112
00:04:11,512 --> 00:04:14,059
train coupler designed to be the weak

113
00:04:14,059 --> 00:04:14,472
point?

114
00:04:14,652 --> 00:04:15,897
It is the knuckle,

115
00:04:15,897 --> 00:04:18,662
the curled piece that locks two couplers

116
00:04:18,662 --> 00:04:19,285
together.

117
00:04:19,465 --> 00:04:21,796
If the force on a train becomes

118
00:04:21,796 --> 00:04:24,202
too high, something has to give.

119
00:04:24,382 --> 00:04:27,163
Railways would much rather that it be

120
00:04:27,163 --> 00:04:28,291
a small, cheap,

121
00:04:28,291 --> 00:04:30,997
replaceable part than the frame of a

122
00:04:30,997 --> 00:04:33,403
car or the coupler shank itself.

123
00:04:33,583 --> 00:04:35,955
So the knuckle is often the part

124
00:04:35,955 --> 00:04:36,845
that breaks.

125
00:04:37,195 --> 00:04:38,922
When a knuckle breaks,

126
00:04:38,922 --> 00:04:40,492
the train separates.

127
00:04:40,672 --> 00:04:43,140
The air brake hose pulls apart,

128
00:04:43,140 --> 00:04:44,652
the pressure drops,

129
00:04:44,652 --> 00:04:47,439
and every car brakes automatically,

130
00:04:47,439 --> 00:04:49,031
so both halves stop.

131
00:04:49,211 --> 00:04:51,696
Then a crew member has to walk

132
00:04:51,696 --> 00:04:53,932
back, sometimes a long way,

133
00:04:53,932 --> 00:04:56,003
carrying a spare knuckle.

134
00:04:56,183 --> 00:05:00,054
A spare knuckle weighs about eighty pounds,

135
00:05:00,054 --> 00:05:02,574
around thirty-six kilograms,

136
00:05:02,574 --> 00:05:06,445
and locomotives often carry one for exactly

137
00:05:06,445 --> 00:05:07,526
this reason.

138
00:05:07,876 --> 00:05:10,364
The idea of a planned weak point

139
00:05:10,364 --> 00:05:13,007
appears everywhere in engineering.

140
00:05:13,187 --> 00:05:15,484
Fuses in electrical systems,

141
00:05:15,484 --> 00:05:17,370
shear pins in machines,

142
00:05:17,370 --> 00:05:19,584
and the knuckle in a train.

143
00:05:19,764 --> 00:05:21,777
When something must fail,

144
00:05:21,777 --> 00:05:23,549
you choose what fails.

145
00:05:23,729 --> 00:05:26,219
It is better to replace a small

146
00:05:26,219 --> 00:05:28,870
piece of steel by the tracks than

147
00:05:28,870 --> 00:05:31,360
to repair a broken car or clean

148
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:32,646
up a derailment.

149
00:05:32,996 --> 00:05:35,111
Why is there a small box with

150
00:05:35,111 --> 00:05:37,446
a blinking red light on the last

151
00:05:37,446 --> 00:05:39,416
car of many freight trains?

152
00:05:39,596 --> 00:05:42,145
It is an end of train device,

153
00:05:42,145 --> 00:05:45,749
nicknamed FRED, flashing rear end device.

154
00:05:45,929 --> 00:05:47,925
It hangs on the rear coupler and

155
00:05:47,925 --> 00:05:49,610
connects to the brake pipe.

156
00:05:49,790 --> 00:05:52,193
It measures the air pressure at the

157
00:05:52,193 --> 00:05:54,253
back of the train and sends it

158
00:05:54,253 --> 00:05:56,107
by radio to the locomotive,

159
00:05:56,107 --> 00:05:58,168
and its light marks the end of

160
00:05:58,168 --> 00:06:00,297
the train for anyone behind it.

161
00:06:00,647 --> 00:06:03,059
Many of these devices also work in

162
00:06:03,059 --> 00:06:04,478
the other direction.

163
00:06:04,658 --> 00:06:07,976
If the engineer needs an emergency stop,

164
00:06:07,976 --> 00:06:10,796
the rear device can open the brake

165
00:06:10,796 --> 00:06:12,538
pipe at the back too,

166
00:06:12,538 --> 00:06:15,773
so the emergency brake starts from both

167
00:06:15,773 --> 00:06:17,266
ends of the train.

168
00:06:17,446 --> 00:06:19,777
That matters if something blocks the air

169
00:06:19,777 --> 00:06:20,593
in the middle.

170
00:06:20,773 --> 00:06:22,436
In the United States,

171
00:06:22,436 --> 00:06:25,842
rules require two-way devices on many heavy

172
00:06:25,842 --> 00:06:27,030
freight trains.

173
00:06:27,381 --> 00:06:30,434
This little box replaced something much bigger.

174
00:06:30,614 --> 00:06:32,488
For more than a century,

175
00:06:32,488 --> 00:06:34,752
the end of a train was marked

176
00:06:34,752 --> 00:06:37,406
by a whole car with people inside.

177
00:06:37,586 --> 00:06:39,427
A box with a battery,

178
00:06:39,427 --> 00:06:41,970
a light and a radio took over

179
00:06:41,970 --> 00:06:42,671
its job.

180
00:06:42,851 --> 00:06:44,707
It is one of the reasons a

181
00:06:44,707 --> 00:06:47,561
freight train that once needed five crew

182
00:06:47,561 --> 00:06:49,631
members can now run with two.

183
00:06:49,982 --> 00:06:53,017
Why did the caboose disappear from freight

184
00:06:53,017 --> 00:06:53,523
trains?

185
00:06:53,703 --> 00:06:56,101
The caboose was the crew's car at

186
00:06:56,101 --> 00:06:57,627
the end of the train.

187
00:06:57,807 --> 00:07:01,092
Conductors and brakemen rode in it,

188
00:07:01,092 --> 00:07:04,472
did paperwork, ate and even slept on

189
00:07:04,472 --> 00:07:05,504
long trips.

190
00:07:05,684 --> 00:07:08,040
Many had a raised window on the

191
00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:09,712
roof, called a cupola,

192
00:07:09,712 --> 00:07:12,220
so the crew could watch the whole

193
00:07:12,220 --> 00:07:15,335
train for smoke from overheated bearings,

194
00:07:15,335 --> 00:07:18,223
shifting loads, or dragging equipment.

195
00:07:18,573 --> 00:07:21,765
Then technology took over its jobs.

196
00:07:21,945 --> 00:07:24,963
Detectors beside the tracks started to check

197
00:07:24,963 --> 00:07:27,158
bearings and dragging equipment.

198
00:07:27,338 --> 00:07:29,953
End of train devices replaced the lights

199
00:07:29,953 --> 00:07:31,587
and the brake monitoring.

200
00:07:31,767 --> 00:07:34,333
Radios replaced hand signals.

201
00:07:34,513 --> 00:07:36,493
In the nineteen eighties,

202
00:07:36,493 --> 00:07:39,899
railways and unions in North America agreed

203
00:07:39,899 --> 00:07:42,750
to run most trains without cabooses,

204
00:07:42,750 --> 00:07:46,077
and they disappeared from most main lines.

205
00:07:46,427 --> 00:07:49,586
Cabooses still exist in a few places,

206
00:07:49,586 --> 00:07:52,745
for example on some local trains that

207
00:07:52,745 --> 00:07:55,222
need a place for crew to ride

208
00:07:55,222 --> 00:07:57,015
while switching cars.

209
00:07:57,195 --> 00:08:00,190
Many old ones became museums,

210
00:08:00,190 --> 00:08:03,081
cafes, or even small hotels.

211
00:08:03,261 --> 00:08:05,770
The caboose is a nice example of

212
00:08:05,770 --> 00:08:08,515
how one machine can be replaced not

213
00:08:08,515 --> 00:08:10,004
by another machine,

214
00:08:10,004 --> 00:08:12,435
but by a group of small sensors

215
00:08:12,435 --> 00:08:14,395
spread along the railway.

216
00:08:14,745 --> 00:08:17,935
Why do many diesel locomotives have big

217
00:08:17,935 --> 00:08:20,143
fans and grids on the roof?

218
00:08:20,323 --> 00:08:21,542
They are for braking.

219
00:08:21,722 --> 00:08:25,031
A diesel locomotive is actually electric.

220
00:08:25,211 --> 00:08:28,242
The diesel engine drives a generator,

221
00:08:28,242 --> 00:08:31,190
and electric motors turn the wheels.

222
00:08:31,370 --> 00:08:32,971
When going downhill,

223
00:08:32,971 --> 00:08:35,851
those motors can be switched to work

224
00:08:35,851 --> 00:08:38,812
as generators, which slows the train.

225
00:08:38,992 --> 00:08:41,744
But the electricity has to go somewhere.

226
00:08:41,924 --> 00:08:44,389
It is sent to big resistor grids

227
00:08:44,389 --> 00:08:45,313
on the roof,

228
00:08:45,313 --> 00:08:47,162
which turn it into heat,

229
00:08:47,162 --> 00:08:49,319
and fans blow the heat away.

230
00:08:49,670 --> 00:08:52,001
This is called dynamic braking,

231
00:08:52,001 --> 00:08:54,557
and it is essential on long hills.

232
00:08:54,737 --> 00:08:57,120
If a heavy train used only its

233
00:08:57,120 --> 00:08:59,978
air brakes on a long downhill grade,

234
00:08:59,978 --> 00:09:03,075
the brake shoes could overheat and lose

235
00:09:03,075 --> 00:09:06,728
their grip, a dangerous condition called brake

236
00:09:06,728 --> 00:09:07,125
fade.

237
00:09:07,305 --> 00:09:10,685
Dynamic braking holds the train back without

238
00:09:10,685 --> 00:09:12,683
wearing the brakes at all,

239
00:09:12,683 --> 00:09:15,064
so the air brakes stay cool and

240
00:09:15,064 --> 00:09:15,525
ready.

241
00:09:15,875 --> 00:09:19,418
Electric locomotives can go one step further,

242
00:09:19,418 --> 00:09:22,647
sending the energy back into the overhead

243
00:09:22,647 --> 00:09:24,931
wire for other trains to use,

244
00:09:24,931 --> 00:09:27,844
which is called regenerative braking.

245
00:09:28,024 --> 00:09:30,645
Diesel locomotives usually cannot,

246
00:09:30,645 --> 00:09:32,494
so they burn it as heat.

247
00:09:32,674 --> 00:09:34,894
On a cold winter day in the

248
00:09:34,894 --> 00:09:38,757
mountains, a locomotive using dynamic brakes is

249
00:09:38,757 --> 00:09:40,894
literally heating the sky.

250
00:09:41,245 --> 00:09:44,364
Why are containers on many North American

251
00:09:44,364 --> 00:09:46,190
trains stacked two high?

252
00:09:46,370 --> 00:09:49,605
Because it almost doubles the cargo per

253
00:09:49,605 --> 00:09:53,006
train, using the same locomotives and the

254
00:09:53,006 --> 00:09:53,836
same crew.

255
00:09:54,016 --> 00:09:55,500
To make it possible,

256
00:09:55,500 --> 00:09:57,949
the cars have a low floor between

257
00:09:57,949 --> 00:09:59,879
the wheels, called a well.

258
00:10:00,059 --> 00:10:02,756
The bottom container sits in the well,

259
00:10:02,756 --> 00:10:04,602
almost touching the rails,

260
00:10:04,602 --> 00:10:06,803
and the second one sits on top,

261
00:10:06,803 --> 00:10:09,358
locked with fittings at the corners.

262
00:10:09,709 --> 00:10:12,713
Double stack trains became popular in the

263
00:10:12,713 --> 00:10:15,572
United States in the nineteen eighties.

264
00:10:15,752 --> 00:10:17,702
But they created a new problem.

265
00:10:17,882 --> 00:10:18,706
They are tall.

266
00:10:18,886 --> 00:10:21,527
Many old tunnels and bridges were too

267
00:10:21,527 --> 00:10:21,812
low.

268
00:10:21,992 --> 00:10:24,922
So railways spent large amounts of money

269
00:10:24,922 --> 00:10:28,072
raising tunnel roofs and lowering tracks to

270
00:10:28,072 --> 00:10:29,097
clear the way.

271
00:10:29,277 --> 00:10:32,135
Some lines were rebuilt only to make

272
00:10:32,135 --> 00:10:34,119
room for that second box.

273
00:10:34,469 --> 00:10:37,133
In Europe and many other places,

274
00:10:37,133 --> 00:10:39,547
double stack trains are rare,

275
00:10:39,547 --> 00:10:43,043
because of electric wires above the tracks

276
00:10:43,043 --> 00:10:45,707
and smaller tunnels and bridges.

277
00:10:45,887 --> 00:10:48,886
That is why American freight trains often

278
00:10:48,886 --> 00:10:51,739
look so much taller than European ones.

279
00:10:51,919 --> 00:10:54,171
The shape of a train is decided

280
00:10:54,171 --> 00:10:55,914
not only by engineering,

281
00:10:55,914 --> 00:10:58,312
but by the height of every bridge

282
00:10:58,312 --> 00:10:59,256
on its route.

283
00:10:59,606 --> 00:11:01,737
Why do some railway yards have a

284
00:11:01,737 --> 00:11:03,402
small hill in the middle?

285
00:11:03,582 --> 00:11:04,991
It is a hump yard,

286
00:11:04,991 --> 00:11:07,809
a giant sorting machine that runs on

287
00:11:07,809 --> 00:11:08,435
gravity.

288
00:11:08,615 --> 00:11:11,810
A locomotive slowly pushes a long line

289
00:11:11,810 --> 00:11:13,491
of cars up the hump.

290
00:11:13,671 --> 00:11:14,565
At the top,

291
00:11:14,565 --> 00:11:17,003
cars are uncoupled one by one,

292
00:11:17,003 --> 00:11:19,766
and each rolls down the other side

293
00:11:19,766 --> 00:11:20,660
on its own.

294
00:11:20,840 --> 00:11:23,509
Switches send each car onto the right

295
00:11:23,509 --> 00:11:26,250
track, where it joins other cars going

296
00:11:26,250 --> 00:11:27,981
to the same destination.

297
00:11:28,331 --> 00:11:31,079
Each car rolls at a different speed,

298
00:11:31,079 --> 00:11:32,911
depending on its weight,

299
00:11:32,911 --> 00:11:34,972
its bearings, and the wind.

300
00:11:35,152 --> 00:11:37,343
So the tracks have retarders,

301
00:11:37,343 --> 00:11:39,762
big steel jaws next to the rails

302
00:11:39,762 --> 00:11:42,407
that squeeze the wheels to slow the

303
00:11:42,407 --> 00:11:43,162
cars down.

304
00:11:43,342 --> 00:11:46,092
That loud screeching sound you hear near

305
00:11:46,092 --> 00:11:48,223
a hump yard is the retarders at

306
00:11:48,223 --> 00:11:48,567
work.

307
00:11:48,747 --> 00:11:51,849
Computers measure each car and decide how

308
00:11:51,849 --> 00:11:53,286
hard to squeeze it.

309
00:11:53,637 --> 00:11:55,855
The goal is for each car to

310
00:11:55,855 --> 00:11:58,566
reach the other cars on its track

311
00:11:58,566 --> 00:12:01,031
gently, fast enough to couple,

312
00:12:01,031 --> 00:12:03,578
but not so fast that it damages

313
00:12:03,578 --> 00:12:04,318
anything.

314
00:12:04,498 --> 00:12:06,961
Big hump yards can sort thousands of

315
00:12:06,961 --> 00:12:07,714
cars a day.

316
00:12:07,894 --> 00:12:10,345
Gravity does most of the work,

317
00:12:10,345 --> 00:12:13,286
and steel jaws and computers keep it

318
00:12:13,286 --> 00:12:14,430
under control.

319
00:12:14,780 --> 00:12:18,289
Why does one railway in California make

320
00:12:18,289 --> 00:12:21,258
a full loop and pass over itself?

321
00:12:21,438 --> 00:12:23,490
It is the Tehachapi Loop,

322
00:12:23,490 --> 00:12:26,773
built in the eighteen seventies to climb

323
00:12:26,773 --> 00:12:28,578
a steep mountain pass.

324
00:12:28,758 --> 00:12:31,600
Instead of going straight up a slope

325
00:12:31,600 --> 00:12:33,258
too steep for trains,

326
00:12:33,258 --> 00:12:36,021
the track spirals around a hill and

327
00:12:36,021 --> 00:12:37,837
gains height gradually.

328
00:12:38,017 --> 00:12:41,086
A long freight train crossing it passes

329
00:12:41,086 --> 00:12:42,423
over its own end,

330
00:12:42,423 --> 00:12:44,941
in one of the most famous sights

331
00:12:44,941 --> 00:12:46,121
in railroading.

332
00:12:46,471 --> 00:12:47,823
Even with the loop,

333
00:12:47,823 --> 00:12:49,105
the climb is hard,

334
00:12:49,105 --> 00:12:51,881
so heavy trains often need extra power.

335
00:12:52,061 --> 00:12:52,959
In the past,

336
00:12:52,959 --> 00:12:55,504
railways added helper locomotives,

337
00:12:55,504 --> 00:12:56,852
often at the back,

338
00:12:56,852 --> 00:12:58,948
to push trains up the grade,

339
00:12:58,948 --> 00:13:01,268
and then sent them back down to

340
00:13:01,268 --> 00:13:02,765
help the next train.

341
00:13:02,945 --> 00:13:06,978
Today, distributed power locomotives often do the

342
00:13:06,978 --> 00:13:10,105
same job, already placed in the middle

343
00:13:10,105 --> 00:13:11,339
and at the end.

344
00:13:11,690 --> 00:13:14,929
Grades matter enormously for trains.

345
00:13:15,109 --> 00:13:18,148
Steel wheels on steel rails have low

346
00:13:18,148 --> 00:13:22,116
friction, which makes trains efficient but also

347
00:13:22,116 --> 00:13:24,733
limits how steep a line can be.

348
00:13:24,913 --> 00:13:28,236
Main lines usually stay very gentle,

349
00:13:28,236 --> 00:13:31,004
often a slope of around one or

350
00:13:31,004 --> 00:13:32,112
two percent.

351
00:13:32,292 --> 00:13:34,913
Climbing a mountain with a train is

352
00:13:34,913 --> 00:13:36,111
not about power.

353
00:13:36,291 --> 00:13:38,892
It is about patience and distance.

354
00:13:39,242 --> 00:13:42,729
Why do many locomotives have lights arranged

355
00:13:42,729 --> 00:13:43,839
in a triangle?

356
00:13:44,019 --> 00:13:45,943
The headlight is on top,

357
00:13:45,943 --> 00:13:47,626
and two extra lights,

358
00:13:47,626 --> 00:13:49,229
called ditch lights,

359
00:13:49,229 --> 00:13:51,473
are lower down on each side.

360
00:13:51,653 --> 00:13:54,843
The triangle helps drivers at a crossing

361
00:13:54,843 --> 00:13:57,315
judge how far away the train is

362
00:13:57,315 --> 00:13:59,389
and how fast it is coming,

363
00:13:59,389 --> 00:14:02,500
because the lights seem to spread apart

364
00:14:02,500 --> 00:14:04,493
as the train gets closer.

365
00:14:04,673 --> 00:14:07,090
A single light is very hard to

366
00:14:07,090 --> 00:14:08,540
judge in the dark.

367
00:14:08,890 --> 00:14:10,541
In the United States,

368
00:14:10,541 --> 00:14:14,158
rules adopted in the nineteen nineties require

369
00:14:14,158 --> 00:14:17,697
these auxiliary lights on lead locomotives on

370
00:14:17,697 --> 00:14:18,955
many main lines.

371
00:14:19,135 --> 00:14:22,236
At crossings, the ditch lights often flash,

372
00:14:22,236 --> 00:14:23,678
along with the horn,

373
00:14:23,678 --> 00:14:24,975
to grab attention.

374
00:14:25,155 --> 00:14:29,242
Many accidents at crossings happen because drivers

375
00:14:29,242 --> 00:14:32,267
underestimate the speed of the train,

376
00:14:32,267 --> 00:14:35,210
so making speed visible saves lives.

377
00:14:35,560 --> 00:14:37,774
That is also why a freight train

378
00:14:37,774 --> 00:14:39,159
cannot stop quickly.

379
00:14:39,339 --> 00:14:41,858
A heavy train at full speed can

380
00:14:41,858 --> 00:14:43,646
need more than a mile,

381
00:14:43,646 --> 00:14:46,572
more than one and a half kilometers,

382
00:14:46,572 --> 00:14:47,222
to stop.

383
00:14:47,402 --> 00:14:49,871
By the time the engineer sees a

384
00:14:49,871 --> 00:14:51,305
car on the tracks,

385
00:14:51,305 --> 00:14:53,137
it is usually too late.

386
00:14:53,317 --> 00:14:55,357
The lights and the horn are not

387
00:14:55,357 --> 00:14:56,080
a courtesy.

388
00:14:56,260 --> 00:14:58,529
They are the only brakes that work

389
00:14:58,529 --> 00:15:00,798
fast enough, the ones in the minds

390
00:15:00,798 --> 00:15:02,332
of the people watching.

391
00:15:02,683 --> 00:15:03,995
So here are the ten.

392
00:15:04,175 --> 00:15:06,138
Engines in the middle to share the

393
00:15:06,138 --> 00:15:06,427
load.

394
00:15:06,607 --> 00:15:08,924
Slack that works like an accordion,

395
00:15:08,924 --> 00:15:11,042
and a knuckle designed to break.

396
00:15:11,222 --> 00:15:14,566
A blinking box that replaced the caboose.

397
00:15:14,746 --> 00:15:17,550
Grids that turn braking into heat,

398
00:15:17,550 --> 00:15:20,107
cars that carry two boxes high,

399
00:15:20,107 --> 00:15:23,241
a hill that sorts trains with gravity,

400
00:15:23,241 --> 00:15:25,797
a track that loops over itself,

401
00:15:25,797 --> 00:15:28,684
and a triangle of lights that shows

402
00:15:28,684 --> 00:15:31,406
you how fast the train is coming.

403
00:15:31,756 --> 00:15:34,489
A freight train looks like the simplest

404
00:15:34,489 --> 00:15:35,680
machine on Earth.

405
00:15:35,860 --> 00:15:38,001
It is actually a two mile long

406
00:15:38,001 --> 00:15:40,214
system fighting its own weight.

407
00:15:40,394 --> 00:15:43,347
If you want more machines explained part

408
00:15:43,347 --> 00:15:46,152
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

409
00:15:46,332 --> 00:15:48,259
And tell us in the comments which

410
00:15:48,259 --> 00:15:50,185
machine you want us to take apart

411
00:15:50,185 --> 00:15:50,477
next.
